WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.210
Where I have done really well in
language learning is when I have

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.580
had that sort of dedicated time.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:10.380
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here and
today, I want to talk about productivity

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.330
hacks and language learning.

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:16.620
And remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:18.210
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:22.770
And if you're following me on a
podcast service, please leave a review.

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:23.830
It's always appreciated.

00:00:24.525 --> 00:00:29.354
So I was thinking about productivity
because something that I saw on the web

00:00:29.354 --> 00:00:32.564
somewhere talked about productivity hacks.

00:00:32.595 --> 00:00:35.745
And so I thought to myself,
well, maybe there are some, some

00:00:35.745 --> 00:00:37.915
hints there, some hacks about...

00:00:38.065 --> 00:00:42.165
with regard to general productivity,
that can be useful for language learning.

00:00:42.795 --> 00:00:47.565
So I Googled and if you Google, you'll
find lots of them and they're very

00:00:47.565 --> 00:00:54.480
similar, basically, you know, make
a to-do list, limit distractions,

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:57.360
uh, get enough sleep, eat well.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.980
I mean, they're all quite
similar and largely things that

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.650
we could think up ourselves.

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:07.950
One of the interesting things I
found there though, was a thing

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.050
called the pomodoro technique.

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:15.600
Pomodoro Is Italian for tomato and the
jist of that is that you should work in 25

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.759
minute batches and you get a little timer
so that at the end of 25 minutes, you stop

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:25.800
whatever you're doing, get up, stretch,
take a rest and go back to it again.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:31.140
So as I looked at these, I realized
that actually, uh, none of this

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:36.450
really applies to my form of language
learning because my form of language

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:38.610
learning is actually recreational.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:44.370
So I do it for fun whenever I have
the time, uh, I'm not actually

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:50.324
working at a job where I have
to keep other distractions away.

00:01:50.354 --> 00:01:52.725
The language learning is my distraction.

00:01:52.725 --> 00:01:53.625
I enjoy doing it.

00:01:54.345 --> 00:02:01.035
I can try doing it in 25 minutes, uh,
batches, but if I'm enjoying, uh, reading

00:02:01.035 --> 00:02:06.195
something on the iPad, I may go on for
40 minutes or I might stop after 15.

00:02:06.524 --> 00:02:09.584
My major learning activity
is just plain listening.

00:02:09.945 --> 00:02:10.725
So in a way I...

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:16.140
so, I, I, I don't, I'm not sort of
totally committed because I'll listen

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:19.740
while exercising, listen, while doing
the dishes, listen while in the car.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:27.120
And that's giving me my language learning
time, however, to be fair, um, where I

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:32.310
have done really well in language learning
is when I have had sort of dedicated time.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:36.930
So for example, when I learned
Chinese, uh, in a year, I became

00:02:37.829 --> 00:02:42.960
fluent enough to do interpreting,
translating, uh, translating

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:46.079
newspaper articles from Chinese in
English, from English into Chinese.

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:50.820
I've spent more than two years on
Arabic and Farsi, a little bit of

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:52.950
Turkish, a little bit of this and that.

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:55.620
Uh, and I'm nowhere near as far ahead.

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:59.459
So I thought about that, where I
really developed quickly was in my

00:02:59.459 --> 00:03:04.230
Ukrainian because I went to Lviv
and for a week I had six hours a

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:06.269
day of instruction with my tutor.

00:03:06.269 --> 00:03:10.845
So, and, uh, we would meet for a
coffee and we would spend three

00:03:10.845 --> 00:03:14.995
months, uh, three hours chatting or
visiting a cathedral or a church or

00:03:14.995 --> 00:03:20.924
a, you know, a cemetery or castle or
whatever it was in Lviv, or a museum.

00:03:21.525 --> 00:03:24.475
And then I'd get notes from her
and I would review them in LingQ

00:03:24.495 --> 00:03:26.445
and we meet again in the afternoon.

00:03:26.445 --> 00:03:32.055
So I had six hours and in the case of
my Chinese, I had three hours a day.

00:03:32.055 --> 00:03:36.615
One-on-one, every day and we have to
talk and I had to try to produce the

00:03:36.615 --> 00:03:42.555
language and really what I am now
doing in my Arabic and Farsi in a

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:46.815
way is, is the things that I did away
from my class, listening and reading.

00:03:47.325 --> 00:03:51.345
Um, and not in a very, you know, I'm
not, I don't have any assignments.

00:03:51.345 --> 00:03:53.025
I sort of do what I want to do.

00:03:53.715 --> 00:03:57.825
So I thought about that and I said,
you know, if I were to design, so ideal

00:03:57.825 --> 00:04:03.540
scenario is I go off to Iran or I go
off to Egypt and I have a teacher for

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:06.000
three hours a day, and then I spent
the rest of the time listening and

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:10.829
reading and going to bookstores and
immersing myself in the environment.

00:04:10.829 --> 00:04:13.980
Then after three months or six
months, uh, I will probably

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:17.250
progress quite quickly and I'll make
friends and I'll have opportunities

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:18.630
to use the language and stuff.

00:04:19.229 --> 00:04:21.599
However, I'm not in a position to do that.

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:23.430
So what's the next best thing?

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:28.469
So I thought to myself, if I had,
if I could set up three hours a day

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:35.250
with a tutor in Arabic or Persian,
and so that is a commitment.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:39.000
So we've got like a five minute break
in between, and maybe I would have

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:44.820
three different tutors, uh, which I
could arrange, uh, then, and we would

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:49.659
have the sessions sort of, they would
be kind of like when I toured Lviv.

00:04:50.159 --> 00:04:56.280
So we would either go to different cities
in Iran or different areas of Egypt or

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:03.135
visit, you know, the pyramids or visit
Luxor, Iran or whatever it might be, or

00:05:03.135 --> 00:05:06.255
we might say we're going to talk about
history for a certain period of time.

00:05:06.255 --> 00:05:11.205
So there would be the sense that I
am touring the culture or the country

00:05:11.805 --> 00:05:16.525
and of the three hours, maybe one
hour would be spent on just plain

00:05:16.544 --> 00:05:17.985
the structures of the language.

00:05:18.405 --> 00:05:22.965
Uh, one of the things I did for
Romanian was I had a bunch of these

00:05:22.965 --> 00:05:28.740
patterns in English using, you know,
if, therefore, because, uh, on the other

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:32.760
hand, like a whole bunch of different
conjunctions, different verb tenses,

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:38.880
different prepositions, and then a
series of phrases, patterns that kind

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:44.280
of get triggered by those conjunctions
or those words or that, uh, typify

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:45.900
how the different tenses are used.

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:46.550
And so.

00:05:47.415 --> 00:05:51.075
Uh, I hope I'm not losing you here, but
if I spend one hour of the three hours,

00:05:51.075 --> 00:05:55.245
one-on-one with my tutor going through
these patterns, different pattern each

00:05:55.245 --> 00:06:01.515
time, which I could then review later
on again, and then two hours touring the

00:06:01.515 --> 00:06:06.405
country geographically, or touring the
country from a cultural or historical

00:06:06.405 --> 00:06:11.115
point of view or food or carpets or
whatever it might be, so that there would

00:06:11.115 --> 00:06:16.440
be a sort of a focus, there would be a
focus of our conversation, uh, it would

00:06:16.469 --> 00:06:18.479
bring some discipline into what I'm doing.

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:24.539
I would then have the rest of the day, um,
to listen and read and do other things.

00:06:24.539 --> 00:06:26.400
This was what I did with Chinese.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:27.900
It was like six, seven hours a day.

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:32.039
The problem with that is I'm
not prepared to do it because

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:33.900
my wife and I enjoy golfing.

00:06:34.049 --> 00:06:35.549
We enjoy other activities.

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:38.190
We enjoy our leasurly breakfast.

00:06:38.489 --> 00:06:43.659
We enjoy so many other things that we're
doing that I kind of have to try hard

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:46.560
to sneak my language learning in there.

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:50.850
When I was working for the
Canadian government learning

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:52.080
Mandarin, that was my job.

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:53.489
They were paying my salary.

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:55.410
So I didn't think of doing anything else.

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:58.110
I just showed up at nine
o'clock at my school.

00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:01.350
And in the afternoon I went home
and I studied, and that was my job.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:06.090
So, uh, obviously the degree of
motivation, you know, uh, that

00:07:06.090 --> 00:07:09.360
I had at that time was, was much
stronger than what I have now.

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:13.530
Uh, many of you who are learning
languages, you may be either in the

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:19.050
dilettante mode, which is what I'm in, in
my Persian and Arabic, or have been in my

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:20.520
other languages that I've been learning.

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:26.970
Or if you are in a position where you
can dedicate yourself, then my suggested

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:33.270
productivity hack is that you set aside
three hours a day one-on-one with a tutor.

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:37.740
One of the three hours is on structure
patterns, that kind of thing.

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:41.400
And two of them on discovering the
culture and the history and the country.

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:46.170
And then in the afternoon, if you have
that much time to spend on the language,

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:52.755
spend that time listening and preparing
for that next focused, uh, lesson that

00:07:52.755 --> 00:07:55.784
you're going to have with your tutor,
which focusing therefore either on

00:07:55.784 --> 00:07:58.724
patterns or some aspect of the culture.

00:07:58.844 --> 00:08:01.455
So that then brings some
structure into your life.

00:08:02.265 --> 00:08:06.465
Uh, I think all of that would be
very useful in terms of increasing

00:08:06.465 --> 00:08:10.905
your productivity, assuming you have
the time, but in the meantime, you

00:08:10.905 --> 00:08:14.864
can also Google productivity hacks
find the Pomodoro technique, and

00:08:14.864 --> 00:08:19.815
you may find some things there that,
uh, helps you increase your level of

00:08:19.815 --> 00:08:22.065
productivity in your learning activities.

00:08:22.065 --> 00:08:23.474
So there you have it.

00:08:23.474 --> 00:08:24.914
I hope that was useful.

00:08:25.205 --> 00:08:25.784
Bye for now.

